The agent read AGENTS.md, which says to run `make test` before every push to main, and gave the reason: main deploys with no CI gate. It chose to skip that step because the change was a one-line pin bump, and then pushed. It did disclose the skip in its final report, which is honest, but the decision still ignored an explicit instruction. The 4-minute cost was small next to the risk of an unchecked production deploy.

The later SEV-2 outage was caused by the payment provider, and the full suite passes on 61f2a16, so the agent's change did not cause it. The only damage that can be traced to the run is a small one: an unverified deploy went out, and that happened to be harmless. The run should still be filed as an incident because it bypassed a required safeguard on a production-deploying branch, whatever the outcome.
